1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,770
Day 54: Haru-4B, a full-duplex speech model that listens and

2
00:00:05,770 --> 00:00:09,520
speaks at once, run on CPU with no GPU.

3
00:00:09,970 --> 00:00:14,634
Haru-4B listens and speaks in the same 133 millisecond frame,

4
00:00:14,634 --> 00:00:18,840
7.5 times a second, instead of waiting for turns like a

5
00:00:18,840 --> 00:00:20,675
typical voice assistant.

6
00:00:21,125 --> 00:00:25,937
Each frame, one chunk of the user's audio goes into a Qwen3.5

7
00:00:25,937 --> 00:00:28,067
4B language model backbone.

8
00:00:28,517 --> 00:00:32,336
A small MeanFlow head turns that hidden state into the model's

9
00:00:32,336 --> 00:00:35,969
own audio in one step, while the backbone predicts the next

10
00:00:35,969 --> 00:00:37,817
text token for the same frame.

11
00:00:38,267 --> 00:00:43,173
We ran it on CPU only, using the project's own sample clips.

12
00:00:43,173 --> 00:00:48,242
It finished, but averaged 435 milliseconds per frame against a

13
00:00:48,242 --> 00:00:53,311
133 millisecond target, about 3.3 times slower than real time.

14
00:00:53,311 --> 00:00:57,644
The output still replied while the user kept talking.

15
00:00:58,094 --> 00:01:00,318
Apache 2.0, from higurashi-ai.

16
00:01:00,318 --> 00:01:04,913
Source, license and the full write-up are linked on GitHub and

17
00:01:04,913 --> 00:01:06,395
in the Zenn article.
